00:07:49.270 --> 00:07:53.790
and allows them to spot trends
and improvements and things

00:07:53.790 --> 00:07:55.130
like that.

00:07:55.130 --> 00:07:57.270
So the current
server-side stack for me

00:07:57.270 --> 00:08:01.680
includes Python and Django
with some customized forms.

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:04.240
This is deployed with
Docker and Kubernetes

00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:06.230
on Google Container Engine.

00:08:06.230 --> 00:08:12.890
I'm using persistent
storage via the Datastore.

00:08:12.890 --> 00:08:15.440
And I'm using Google Charts for
the dashboard at the moment,

00:08:15.440 --> 00:08:18.290
making use of the Python library
that's available for charts,

00:08:18.290 --> 00:08:20.790
which was quite convenient.

00:08:20.790 --> 00:08:23.660
I'm also looking to use
material charts at a later point

00:08:23.660 --> 00:08:26.270
when that moves out of beta.

00:08:26.270 --> 00:08:29.220
The client-side stack
for the data collection

00:08:29.220 --> 00:08:32.260
consists of Django web
forms with Material Design

00:08:32.260 --> 00:08:36.100
Lite to put some material
design on the web for me,

00:08:36.100 --> 00:08:39.860
focusing on mobile use
case here and fluid design.

00:08:39.860 --> 00:08:42.490
So I chose the stack
I chose, because I

00:08:42.490 --> 00:08:43.929
am most familiar with Python.

00:08:43.929 --> 00:08:47.550
My background involves
using Python throughout.

00:08:47.550 --> 00:08:51.000
And I quite like the concept of
deploying Docker and container

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:54.490
management solutions such as
Kubernetes over virtual machine

00:08:54.490 --> 00:08:57.560
deployment-style deployments.

00:08:57.560 --> 00:09:00.390
And Container Engine was my
choice, because, at the moment,

00:09:00.390 --> 00:09:03.540
it seems to be the best
place for hosted Kubernetes

00:09:03.540 --> 00:09:06.214
as far as I've seen out there.

00:09:06.214 --> 00:09:07.130
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

