00:09:33.660 --> 00:09:35.820
it's going to take a long time.

00:09:35.820 --> 00:09:37.320
What about down there?

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:38.200
DALE HUMBY: Sure.

00:09:38.200 --> 00:09:40.690
So a particularly
interesting thing for me

00:09:40.690 --> 00:09:44.080
is within South Africa the
connectivity scenario has

00:09:44.080 --> 00:09:47.160
gotten a bit better
over the years.

00:09:47.160 --> 00:09:49.820
I couldn't say the same for
the rest of Africa, sadly.

00:09:49.820 --> 00:09:52.640
Although for me a particular
challenge is mobile data

00:09:52.640 --> 00:09:55.980
costs still remain quite
high within South Africa.

00:09:55.980 --> 00:09:59.520
And the impact of that is felt
not just by consumers, but also

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:02.550
by small- to
medium-sized businesses.

00:10:02.550 --> 00:10:04.940
A recent example of
having run into that-- I

00:10:04.940 --> 00:10:07.160
was doing some work
for a company that

00:10:07.160 --> 00:10:10.410
had about 150 sales
reps out in the field.

00:10:10.410 --> 00:10:14.300
And these sales reps had
tablet devices with SIM cards

00:10:14.300 --> 00:10:16.450
for connectivity, and
they were doing data

00:10:16.450 --> 00:10:18.670
capturing using these devices.

00:10:18.670 --> 00:10:21.870
And on one fine day, a
frenzy of support cases

00:10:21.870 --> 00:10:25.060
came in whereby all these
devices stopped working.

00:10:25.060 --> 00:10:28.740
And it turned out to be that
the company had contracted

00:10:28.740 --> 00:10:31.820
a package for all these SIM
cards from a mobile network

00:10:31.820 --> 00:10:34.740
operator, and they ended
up getting their data cap

00:10:34.740 --> 00:10:36.300
for this package.

00:10:36.300 --> 00:10:40.070
And so, basically, all the
sales reps jobs came to an end

00:10:40.070 --> 00:10:41.680
for the rest of that day.

00:10:41.680 --> 00:10:43.420
So that was quite
interesting to see.

00:10:43.420 --> 00:10:45.360
I mean they had to go
and investigate the data

00:10:45.360 --> 00:10:48.880
usage afterwards and come up
with plans to try and keep

00:10:48.880 --> 00:10:51.570
costs down, to reduce the
amount of data being used.

00:10:51.570 --> 00:10:54.080
So data cost is
still something being

00:10:54.080 --> 00:10:56.252
felt by businesses
and consumers,

00:10:56.252 --> 00:10:59.620
and it's quite a challenge in
South Africa at the moment.

00:10:59.620 --> 00:11:01.890
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I really
like that you categorize it

00:11:01.890 --> 00:11:03.690
as something
"interesting" to see.

00:11:06.880 --> 00:11:08.530
I'm pretty sure
that the people that

00:11:08.530 --> 00:11:11.300
were on field with tablet
that were completely bricked,

00:11:11.300 --> 00:11:12.840
they were not that happy.

00:11:12.840 --> 00:11:13.990
MARK MANDEL: I'm sure the
word they weren't using

00:11:13.990 --> 00:11:14.656
was interesting.

00:11:16.850 --> 00:11:18.350
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That's really cool.

00:11:18.350 --> 00:11:21.260
What about you, Dale, do
you have any story to share?

00:11:21.260 --> 00:11:22.660
DALE HUMBY: Yeah.

00:11:22.660 --> 00:11:24.980
Most of our business
is up in Africa,

00:11:24.980 --> 00:11:29.570
and definitely South Africa is
the most advanced when it comes

00:11:29.570 --> 00:11:32.310
to services being available.

00:11:32.310 --> 00:11:34.330
A lot of places where
we operate the services

00:11:34.330 --> 00:11:36.340
are incredibly unreliable.

00:11:36.340 --> 00:11:39.830
Electricity can be down for
18 hours a day sometimes.

00:11:39.830 --> 00:11:43.170
So in the evenings you might
have maybe just a few hours

00:11:43.170 --> 00:11:45.250
to charge your phone
or your tablet.

00:11:45.250 --> 00:11:48.087
And that's got to last
you one or two days.

00:11:48.087 --> 00:11:50.420
So we can't just leave our
phones on charge all the time

00:11:50.420 --> 00:11:53.180
and expect them to
carry on working,

00:11:53.180 --> 00:11:55.580
because the power is out a lot.

00:11:55.580 --> 00:11:58.320
Also the mobile networks
are very overloaded,

00:11:58.320 --> 00:12:02.230
and there's limited connection
to the outside world

00:12:02.230 --> 00:12:03.040
very often.

00:12:03.040 --> 00:12:05.710
So you find that in the
evenings the mobile networks

00:12:05.710 --> 00:12:09.820
will prioritize voice
traffic over data traffic.

00:12:09.820 --> 00:12:12.460
So a lot of your websites just
stop working or your web apps

00:12:12.460 --> 00:12:16.140
just stop getting a
data connection through,

00:12:16.140 --> 00:12:18.400
which is really frustrating
if you're trying to use

00:12:18.400 --> 00:12:21.220
Google Maps or something.

00:12:21.220 --> 00:12:25.230
And sometimes a whole
fiber cable will go down.

00:12:25.230 --> 00:12:27.850
And I think over the
last year sometime--

00:12:27.850 --> 00:12:30.402
I can't remember exactly
which month it was--

00:12:30.402 --> 00:12:33.960
the entire country of Nigeria
was offline for a day,

00:12:33.960 --> 00:12:35.370
because the fiber went down.

00:12:35.370 --> 00:12:38.280
And when a country is
isolated like that,

00:12:38.280 --> 00:12:40.530
no news can get
in or out easily,

00:12:40.530 --> 00:12:42.422
and it's very frustrating.

00:12:42.422 --> 00:12:43.130
MARK MANDEL: Wow.

00:12:43.130 --> 00:12:47.060
And do you find as well
that device penetration is

00:12:47.060 --> 00:12:48.030
quite different there?

00:12:48.030 --> 00:12:51.319
I mean, obviously-- I know
living here, technically, well,

00:12:51.319 --> 00:12:53.110
living here in the
bubble in San Francisco,

00:12:53.110 --> 00:12:54.720
I've got the latest
Android device.

00:12:54.720 --> 00:12:56.110
I've got a nice fast laptop.

00:12:56.110 --> 00:12:58.440
Do you find that you get a
different device penetration

00:12:58.440 --> 00:12:59.282
as well?

00:12:59.282 --> 00:12:59.990
DALE HUMBY: Yeah.

00:12:59.990 --> 00:13:02.730
A lot of people still
have feature phones.

00:13:02.730 --> 00:13:06.560
And you know the first time
they get an Android device,

00:13:06.560 --> 00:13:09.980
it's quite a challenge
to try and teach people

00:13:09.980 --> 00:13:11.870
what a touchscreen is.

00:13:11.870 --> 00:13:13.790
They've never used a
touchscreen before.

00:13:13.790 --> 00:13:17.400
And even with the latest trends
in [? icontariel ?] design,

00:13:17.400 --> 00:13:21.480
where the icons are
very flat, the buttons

00:13:21.480 --> 00:13:23.930
don't necessarily look
like buttons anymore.

00:13:23.930 --> 00:13:30.160
And trying to teach guys
what the different actions do

00:13:30.160 --> 00:13:31.860
is quite a challenge
when they haven't

00:13:31.860 --> 00:13:37.530
been through the last 10 years
of user interface development.

00:13:37.530 --> 00:13:39.790
And also the feature
phones that now

00:13:39.790 --> 00:13:41.880
are going displaced by
cheaper Android phones

00:13:41.880 --> 00:13:44.880
are still-- those Android
phones are the very low end

00:13:44.880 --> 00:13:48.610
ones so in some cases
still running like KitKat

00:13:48.610 --> 00:13:52.210
from a few years ago.

00:13:52.210 --> 00:13:54.450
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So how
do you deal with this?

00:13:54.450 --> 00:13:57.530
Is there any kind of specific
technology or some kind

00:13:57.530 --> 00:14:01.530
of practices that you apply to
make sure that, one, the data

00:14:01.530 --> 00:14:05.040
restrictions are not
a big problem as I

00:14:05.040 --> 00:14:06.380
think they could be?

00:14:06.380 --> 00:14:11.300
And, two, that , people that
don't have the background using

00:14:11.300 --> 00:14:15.307
smartphones for years are
able to still use your apps?

00:14:15.307 --> 00:14:17.390
DALE HUMBY: Hiren, do you
want to answer this one?

00:14:17.390 --> 00:14:18.140
HIREN PATEL: Yeah.

00:14:18.140 --> 00:14:21.800
I think it's quite
a tricky situation.

00:14:21.800 --> 00:14:24.310
I'm not sure about the
market in the rest of Africa,

00:14:24.310 --> 00:14:26.030
but in South Africa,
specifically, I

00:14:26.030 --> 00:14:28.830
know there's been a whole
bunch of cheap smartphones

00:14:28.830 --> 00:14:31.630
that have recently
hit the market.

00:14:31.630 --> 00:14:34.017
Particularly, I
think, I'm not sure

00:14:34.017 --> 00:14:35.600
if it's a South
African culture thing,

00:14:35.600 --> 00:14:38.460
but people sort of tend to
get used to using the devices

00:14:38.460 --> 00:14:42.200
and pick it up quickly enough.

00:14:42.200 --> 00:14:46.210
I haven't really worked in
the space of providing apps

00:14:46.210 --> 00:14:50.950
in terms of providing them on
the place for our consumers

00:14:50.950 --> 00:14:51.850
as such.

00:14:51.850 --> 00:14:53.940
I've done more
business-sided apps.

00:14:53.940 --> 00:14:57.502
And the users of my
apps are I wouldn't

00:14:57.502 --> 00:14:59.460
say people who are using
it for the first time.

00:14:59.460 --> 00:15:02.597
So I haven't really battled
in that space all that much.

00:15:02.597 --> 00:15:03.430
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:15:03.430 --> 00:15:04.763
What about what about you, Dale?

00:15:04.763 --> 00:15:08.210
You mentioned that one of the
things that you do-- if I'm not

00:15:08.210 --> 00:15:12.370
mistaking it now-- is you
provide an application

00:15:12.370 --> 00:15:14.630
so people can buy electricity?

00:15:14.630 --> 00:15:16.960
DALE HUMBY: Actually,
we provide a whole piece

00:15:16.960 --> 00:15:18.020
of our own hardware.

00:15:18.020 --> 00:15:21.010
A point-of-sale terminal that
we developed from the ground up.

00:15:21.010 --> 00:15:22.385
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Oh, interesting.

00:15:22.385 --> 00:15:25.720
DALE HUMBY: And because a lot
of people only potentially

00:15:25.720 --> 00:15:29.540
semi-literate, and they're
not used to smartphones,

00:15:29.540 --> 00:15:32.730
we purposefully avoided
that paradigm for now

00:15:32.730 --> 00:15:37.430
and gone for devices
that have either just

00:15:37.430 --> 00:15:41.890
buttons and no screen or a
very simple screen with very

00:15:41.890 --> 00:15:46.920
obvious menus and buttons to
try and educate them in this.

00:15:46.920 --> 00:15:49.940
And we found that that's
working really well.

00:15:49.940 --> 00:15:52.670
And with some of our
more advanced clients,

00:15:52.670 --> 00:15:55.949
moving them over to
Android terminals.

00:15:55.949 --> 00:15:56.990
MARK MANDEL: Interesting.

00:15:56.990 --> 00:16:02.110
So since it is the Google
Cloud Platform Podcast,

00:16:02.110 --> 00:16:04.660
do these-- well, I don't know
if they're unique challenges,

00:16:04.660 --> 00:16:06.540
but do these
particular challenges,

00:16:06.540 --> 00:16:09.459
do they dictate what sort of
backend solutions that you use?

00:16:09.459 --> 00:16:10.750
You've talked about App Engine.

00:16:10.750 --> 00:16:13.550
You've both talked
about Kubernetes.

00:16:13.550 --> 00:16:16.320
Is it something that is
affected by that sort of stuff?

00:16:16.320 --> 00:16:17.612
Or is it not necessarily?

00:16:17.612 --> 00:16:18.320
HIREN PATEL: Yes.

00:16:18.320 --> 00:16:20.630
So for me coming from
Google App Engine,

00:16:20.630 --> 00:16:23.010
I think I choose Google
Cloud Platform products

00:16:23.010 --> 00:16:25.800
more for the tools and
the developer convenience

00:16:25.800 --> 00:16:27.360
it gives me.

00:16:27.360 --> 00:16:29.960
If the product or the platform
come with technologies

00:16:29.960 --> 00:16:32.650
that help our specific
issues, then that's

00:16:32.650 --> 00:16:34.830
kind of a bonus for me.

00:16:34.830 --> 00:16:38.330
But I choose them more for
the convenience and the tools

00:16:38.330 --> 00:16:41.682
and the experience
of development.

00:16:41.682 --> 00:16:43.070
MARK MANDEL: That's fair enough.

00:16:43.070 --> 00:16:45.930
DALE HUMBY: And I tend
to echo what Hiren said.

00:16:45.930 --> 00:16:49.120
When we set out we had a
big challenge ahead of us

00:16:49.120 --> 00:16:51.950
to build our hardware.

00:16:51.950 --> 00:16:55.460
And we wanted a backend that
we could just rely on and not

00:16:55.460 --> 00:16:57.862
have to spend months
setting up, so that's

00:16:57.862 --> 00:16:59.695
why we chose App Engine,
because it was just

00:16:59.695 --> 00:17:03.740
the platform that we could write
our Python code and deploy.

00:17:03.740 --> 00:17:06.300
And that's stood us in
incredibly good stead

00:17:06.300 --> 00:17:09.210
for the last four or five
years that we've been on it.

00:17:09.210 --> 00:17:13.294
And, yeah, I think--
what other reasons have

00:17:13.294 --> 00:17:16.452
I got for App Engine or Cloud?

00:17:16.452 --> 00:17:18.910
I think the tooling that's been
developed around especially

00:17:18.910 --> 00:17:21.550
with monitoring and logging
has been a huge help

00:17:21.550 --> 00:17:26.319
as we've grown-- got bigger
to allow us to find problems

00:17:26.319 --> 00:17:30.186
with our stack with our
application code specifically.

00:17:30.186 --> 00:17:32.790
We've got dashboards
throughout the office with all

00:17:32.790 --> 00:17:35.900
the monitoring on and so we
can see in real time what's

00:17:35.900 --> 00:17:36.930
happening on our system.

00:17:36.930 --> 00:17:39.920
And it's just a
great benefit to us

00:17:39.920 --> 00:17:42.930
that we don't have to worry
about keeping the servers up

00:17:42.930 --> 00:17:46.910
and running when we've got
thousands of points of sales

00:17:46.910 --> 00:17:49.061
to keep running, which
is challenge enough.

00:17:49.061 --> 00:17:50.102
MARK MANDEL: Interesting.

00:17:50.102 --> 00:17:50.602
All right.

00:17:50.602 --> 00:17:52.100
Very cool.

00:17:52.100 --> 00:17:57.060
So what sort of
upcoming technologies

00:17:57.060 --> 00:17:59.660
that you gentlemen
particularly are excited

00:17:59.660 --> 00:18:03.080
by, either in general
or maybe him in relation

00:18:03.080 --> 00:18:05.120
to the challenges you're
facing there in Africa?

00:18:05.120 --> 00:18:05.870
HIREN PATEL: Cool.

00:18:05.870 --> 00:18:08.280
So, yeah, with Africa
being mostly mobile,

00:18:08.280 --> 00:18:11.930
and the mobile being mostly
web, there are two upcoming

00:18:11.930 --> 00:18:14.650
technologies in the web space
which I'm quite excited about,

00:18:14.650 --> 00:18:17.360
which I think will affect
a number of us here.

00:18:17.360 --> 00:18:20.520
The first one is Service
Worker for the web, which

00:18:20.520 --> 00:18:23.970
is an exciting new
browser standard that's

00:18:23.970 --> 00:18:28.149
coming together to offer rich
offline and powerful caching.

00:18:28.149 --> 00:18:29.690
And I think what
that's going to give

00:18:29.690 --> 00:18:33.430
us is web app experience
that's not only fast,

00:18:33.430 --> 00:18:35.990
but that's going to use
less data, two things which

00:18:35.990 --> 00:18:38.482
are key problems for us here.

00:18:38.482 --> 00:18:40.440
I'm looking to use this
in the current project,

00:18:40.440 --> 00:18:44.680
as well, for uses such as
offline kiosks or kiosks where

00:18:44.680 --> 00:18:48.050
the internet connection
may be flaky.

00:18:48.050 --> 00:18:50.364
And the second piece
of web technology

00:18:50.364 --> 00:18:51.780
that I'm really
looking forward to

00:18:51.780 --> 00:18:56.430
is the HTTP save data
client hint header.

00:18:56.430 --> 00:18:58.550
This lets the
browser or the client

00:18:58.550 --> 00:19:01.750
explicitly tell the server
that it wants to save data,

00:19:01.750 --> 00:19:05.130
and the server can serve down
different resources altogether

00:19:05.130 --> 00:19:07.180
based on this.

00:19:07.180 --> 00:19:09.150
I think the server
sending down resources

00:19:09.150 --> 00:19:11.790
based on connection speeds
or device types, which

00:19:11.790 --> 00:19:14.520
we have today is
great, but I feel

00:19:14.520 --> 00:19:16.250
like there's a real
need for us here

00:19:16.250 --> 00:19:21.330
to be able to explicitly use
data saving features even

00:19:21.330 --> 00:19:24.620
on fast connections, because
of the prices of our data.

00:19:24.620 --> 00:19:26.720
So I read somewhere
recently that 50%

00:19:26.720 --> 00:19:29.700
of smartphone users in India
switch their data connection

00:19:29.700 --> 00:19:30.200
off.

00:19:30.200 --> 00:19:31.840
They don't keep it
on all the time.

00:19:31.840 --> 00:19:34.219
And my first thought was,
well, I do the same thing here.

00:19:34.219 --> 00:19:35.260
MARK MANDEL: Fair enough.

00:19:35.260 --> 00:19:37.925
So you want some useful
ways to be able to say,

00:19:37.925 --> 00:19:39.300
OK, I'm going to
turn on my data,

00:19:39.300 --> 00:19:42.024
grab something, use
it, then turn it off,

00:19:42.024 --> 00:19:43.690
and hopefully I can
still use it in sort

00:19:43.690 --> 00:19:44.995
of like an offline mode.

00:19:44.995 --> 00:19:45.870
HIREN PATEL: Exactly.

00:19:45.870 --> 00:19:49.010
As well as when I am
using it, the server

00:19:49.010 --> 00:19:52.410
should be smart about what it
serves me if I'm out and about,

00:19:52.410 --> 00:19:56.270
and I just want to
pull up some resource

00:19:56.270 --> 00:19:58.780
to get some information
that I don't want the server

00:19:58.780 --> 00:20:01.230
to serve me things that's
going to cost me money

00:20:01.230 --> 00:20:02.384
that it doesn't need to.

00:20:02.384 --> 00:20:04.050
MARK MANDEL: That's
totally fair enough.

00:20:04.050 --> 00:20:05.940
Dale, anything to add?

00:20:05.940 --> 00:20:10.430
DALE HUMBY: The proliferation of
cheap Android phones is great.

00:20:10.430 --> 00:20:12.740
It's really bringing the
cost of hardware down

00:20:12.740 --> 00:20:14.980
and this is got a
massive positive effect

00:20:14.980 --> 00:20:18.120
on consumer
electronics in general,

00:20:18.120 --> 00:20:25.900
not just phones-- like RAM
and Flash, the cellphone GSM

00:20:25.900 --> 00:20:28.030
modules, GPS
modules, all of that

00:20:28.030 --> 00:20:30.820
is so much cheaper than
it was five years ago.

00:20:30.820 --> 00:20:32.750
And that's not just
due to Moore's law,

00:20:32.750 --> 00:20:37.340
it's because the Android
phones and their proliferation

00:20:37.340 --> 00:20:39.960
have meant that these
processes are on the market

00:20:39.960 --> 00:20:42.610
and we can use them in any
source of electronics, which

00:20:42.610 --> 00:20:46.810
has got a huge benefit
for people developing

00:20:46.810 --> 00:20:50.100
tools in Africa who want to
build their own electronics

00:20:50.100 --> 00:20:51.540
and build their own solutions.

00:20:51.540 --> 00:20:53.290
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That is very cool.

00:20:53.290 --> 00:20:55.660
I was right now
checking for something

00:20:55.660 --> 00:20:59.980
that I was curious about,
which is sometimes consumers,

00:20:59.980 --> 00:21:02.875
especially users of
Google Cloud Platform,

00:21:02.875 --> 00:21:06.700
or actually Asia or
Amazon, they consider

00:21:06.700 --> 00:21:09.800
being close to a data center
to be something really, really

00:21:09.800 --> 00:21:12.400
interesting.

00:21:12.400 --> 00:21:13.880
I was looking at the maps.

00:21:13.880 --> 00:21:17.150
You could not be farther
away from a data center,

00:21:17.150 --> 00:21:20.540
literally, from any data center.

00:21:20.540 --> 00:21:23.650
Does that have an impact
on the way you develop too?

00:21:23.650 --> 00:21:26.960
It's not only the fact that from
your customers to your servers

00:21:26.960 --> 00:21:30.480
there may be a delay, but even
from you to a data center,

00:21:30.480 --> 00:21:31.910
there's also delay.

00:21:31.910 --> 00:21:33.289
Is that something that you feel?

00:21:33.289 --> 00:21:35.330
DALE HUMBY: Yeah, we'd
wish Google would build us

00:21:35.330 --> 00:21:38.010
a data center in Africa.

00:21:38.010 --> 00:21:40.070
That would be so much better.

00:21:40.070 --> 00:21:43.990
But in all seriousness,
the connectivity,

00:21:43.990 --> 00:21:49.850
or the lag over the GSM network,
specifically GPRS or EDGE

00:21:49.850 --> 00:21:54.050
is pretty bad anyway, so
the 180 millisecond ping

00:21:54.050 --> 00:21:58.020
time to our data
center in the US

00:21:58.020 --> 00:22:01.140
is not so bad when you
consider that sometimes

00:22:01.140 --> 00:22:04.797
over the mobile network you've
got a three second ping time.

00:22:04.797 --> 00:22:05.630
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:22:05.630 --> 00:22:06.260
Wow.

00:22:06.260 --> 00:22:08.000
DALE HUMBY: You have
to rely on caching.

00:22:08.000 --> 00:22:10.090
And caching for
the speed, but also

00:22:10.090 --> 00:22:12.630
caching if the network
goes down and you still

00:22:12.630 --> 00:22:14.017
want the content locally.

00:22:14.017 --> 00:22:15.600
MARK MANDEL: I'm
trying to find a map.

00:22:15.600 --> 00:22:16.770
I can't seem to find it.

00:22:16.770 --> 00:22:18.060
Obviously, we have our
points of presence,

00:22:18.060 --> 00:22:19.570
so I'm wondering whether we
have one in South Africa.

00:22:19.570 --> 00:22:21.445
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Oh,
that may be interesting.

00:22:21.445 --> 00:22:25.194
DALE HUMBY: Yeah,
Johannesburg's got a pop.

00:22:25.194 --> 00:22:26.110
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:22:26.110 --> 00:22:27.260
That's very interesting.

00:22:27.260 --> 00:22:31.570
And that's interesting that the
same way you optimize a program

00:22:31.570 --> 00:22:34.320
by finding the slowest
part and not trying

00:22:34.320 --> 00:22:36.350
to optimize just randomly.

00:22:36.350 --> 00:22:41.740
Here you've-- a ping on the GSM
network might take up to three

00:22:41.740 --> 00:22:42.810
seconds.

00:22:42.810 --> 00:22:45.580
Yeah, I don't think the problem
is the connectivity to the data

00:22:45.580 --> 00:22:45.710
center.

00:22:45.710 --> 00:22:46.376
MARK MANDEL: No.

00:22:46.376 --> 00:22:48.587
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That's crazy.

00:22:48.587 --> 00:22:50.420
DALE HUMBY: Look, it's
not always like that.

00:22:50.420 --> 00:22:53.120
But it can go like
that sometimes.

00:22:53.120 --> 00:22:54.790
And just everything
goes out when

00:22:54.790 --> 00:22:56.380
you've got such bad ping times.

00:22:56.380 --> 00:22:58.570
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
that is very interesting.

00:22:58.570 --> 00:23:00.560
I know that in here
I really appreciate

00:23:00.560 --> 00:23:03.990
when I can use an application
offline, because it's something

00:23:03.990 --> 00:23:06.010
that can be useful sometimes.

00:23:06.010 --> 00:23:09.820
But it's more like a little plus
that I appreciate rather than,

00:23:09.820 --> 00:23:13.050
oh, yeah, if not, I
cannot really use it.

00:23:13.050 --> 00:23:14.000
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:23:14.000 --> 00:23:16.240
It gives me a little
perspective on-- I

00:23:16.240 --> 00:23:18.130
used to live in
Australia, where I always

00:23:18.130 --> 00:23:20.171
complained about how slow
the bandwidth there is.

00:23:20.171 --> 00:23:22.697
And now I'm like, OK, so
maybe that's not as bad

00:23:22.697 --> 00:23:27.500
as like it was in my mind.

00:23:27.500 --> 00:23:32.430
DALE HUMBY: I think with
the local point of presence

00:23:32.430 --> 00:23:36.580
and the EDGE caches, there
are multiple ones that Google

00:23:36.580 --> 00:23:40.170
supplies around Africa,
so that does help,

00:23:40.170 --> 00:23:42.812
if you sort out your
caching headers correctly

00:23:42.812 --> 00:23:45.466
then some of the static
content is at least faster.

00:23:45.466 --> 00:23:48.280
And everything you just
do through Ajax callbacks.

00:23:48.280 --> 00:23:49.030
HIREN PATEL: Yeah.

00:23:49.030 --> 00:23:49.960
I tend to agree.

00:23:49.960 --> 00:23:52.360
I think it depends very
specifically on the application

00:23:52.360 --> 00:23:53.290
itself.

00:23:53.290 --> 00:23:55.450
And if it can tolerate
a bit of latency,

00:23:55.450 --> 00:23:57.590
then the caching is
generally good enough

00:23:57.590 --> 00:23:59.717
to give the user an
experience that's OK.

00:23:59.717 --> 00:24:01.800
FRANCESC CAMPOY: There's
something related to this

00:24:01.800 --> 00:24:03.735
that we actually
