which is YouTube Red.

00:24:07.890 --> 00:24:11.690
I don't know if you have tried
since YouTube Red allowing

00:24:11.690 --> 00:24:15.480
you to download videos from
YouTube directly for free

00:24:15.480 --> 00:24:17.180
so you can watch them later on.

00:24:17.180 --> 00:24:18.300
MARK MANDEL: Now, it's for
free only in South Africa.

00:24:18.300 --> 00:24:20.020
FRANCESC CAMPOY: For free only
in South Africa, by the way.

00:24:20.020 --> 00:24:22.190
I think it may be in other
countries of Africa too.

00:24:22.190 --> 00:24:23.340
MARK MANDEL: OK, fair enough.

00:24:23.340 --> 00:24:24.135
FRANCESC CAMPOY: But
it's definitely not

00:24:24.135 --> 00:24:25.218
free in the United States.

00:24:25.218 --> 00:24:25.950
MARK MANDEL: No.

00:24:25.950 --> 00:24:27.870
FRANCESC CAMPOY: But
have you tried it?

00:24:27.870 --> 00:24:29.490
HIREN PATEL: I haven't as yet.

00:24:29.490 --> 00:24:33.120
I previously used another
tool, but this is great,

00:24:33.120 --> 00:24:35.900
if if this would allow me
to do it more conveniently,

00:24:35.900 --> 00:24:37.839
I definitely would look into it.

00:24:37.839 --> 00:24:39.880
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Do you
think that it's something

00:24:39.880 --> 00:24:41.930
that your users
could appreciate?

00:24:41.930 --> 00:24:44.130
Like the fact that
it's not only that they

00:24:44.130 --> 00:24:46.540
can use your
application offline,

00:24:46.540 --> 00:24:52.780
but they actually can prepare
for long offline periods

00:24:52.780 --> 00:24:55.712
by preparing, downloading
documents, and so on.

00:24:55.712 --> 00:24:56.920
DALE HUMBY: Yeah, absolutely.

00:24:56.920 --> 00:24:59.759
And if you're at the
office or an internet cafe,

00:24:59.759 --> 00:25:01.300
then you can use
the Wi-Fi connection

00:25:01.300 --> 00:25:02.710
to download all your content.

00:25:02.710 --> 00:25:04.920
And then when you're
roaming around or at home,

00:25:04.920 --> 00:25:06.770
then you don't have to use your
mobile connection at all, which

00:25:06.770 --> 00:25:07.970
saves quite a lot of money.

00:25:07.970 --> 00:25:09.969
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
that is very interesting.

00:25:09.969 --> 00:25:12.471
I would like to know if there's
some other listeners that

00:25:12.471 --> 00:25:14.220
might be using these
kind of technologies,

00:25:14.220 --> 00:25:18.290
these kind of techniques to make
their applications more usable

00:25:18.290 --> 00:25:22.230
really in some other parts
of the world, really.

00:25:22.230 --> 00:25:24.940
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, it's
definitely a great perspective

00:25:24.940 --> 00:25:28.010
in that when you're-- I
think often we tend to think

00:25:28.010 --> 00:25:30.590
of things as totally
always connected,

00:25:30.590 --> 00:25:32.459
just because of where
we are and what we do.

00:25:32.459 --> 00:25:34.250
But thinking about it
from the perspective,

00:25:34.250 --> 00:25:36.780
OK, what happens when
you're disconnected

00:25:36.780 --> 00:25:40.257
on a regular basis, not
just on an airplane.

00:25:40.257 --> 00:25:41.340
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Oh, yeah.

00:25:41.340 --> 00:25:43.890
Not even in airplanes
we're disconnected anymore,

00:25:43.890 --> 00:25:44.598
most of the time.

00:25:47.152 --> 00:25:48.110
MARK MANDEL: Well, yes.

00:25:48.110 --> 00:25:49.490
[INAUDIBLE]

