00:28:00.770 --> 00:28:04.190
So we have a good lead
now into our question

00:28:04.190 --> 00:28:07.270
of the week from our
previous conversation, which

00:28:07.270 --> 00:28:09.834
is sort of about connectivity.

00:28:09.834 --> 00:28:11.250
We sort of got
asked the question,

00:28:11.250 --> 00:28:13.990
so when I'm developing
mobile applications,

00:28:13.990 --> 00:28:16.580
and maybe I have spotty
connectivity, right,

00:28:16.580 --> 00:28:19.120
maybe it drops out
on a regular basis.

00:28:19.120 --> 00:28:21.000
Maybe it's for
people who fly a lot

00:28:21.000 --> 00:28:23.490
or go into places where
they don't necessarily have

00:28:23.490 --> 00:28:25.340
connection to the internet.

00:28:25.340 --> 00:28:27.510
How can Google Cloud
Platform help you

00:28:27.510 --> 00:28:29.334
when you're offline
a lot of the time?

00:28:29.334 --> 00:28:31.000
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
how can the Cloud

00:28:31.000 --> 00:28:34.400
help you when you have
access to the Cloud?

00:28:34.400 --> 00:28:37.810
So surprisingly, the
answer is we have

00:28:37.810 --> 00:28:39.460
actually something really cool.

00:28:39.460 --> 00:28:39.830
MARK MANDEL: Yeah?

00:28:39.830 --> 00:28:40.490
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:28:40.490 --> 00:28:41.920
Which I really did not expect.

00:28:41.920 --> 00:28:44.030
But I was looking
for it while we

00:28:44.030 --> 00:28:46.980
were talking with
Hiren and Dale,

00:28:46.980 --> 00:28:51.590
and I saw that we
have Firebase offline.

00:28:51.590 --> 00:28:52.600
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, OK.

00:28:52.600 --> 00:28:55.214
So Firebase has inbuilt support
for offline capabilities?

00:28:55.214 --> 00:28:56.130
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:28:56.130 --> 00:28:58.860
And there's some of the stuff
that you can do for the web,

00:28:58.860 --> 00:29:02.440
but it's especially powerful
for Android and iOS.

00:29:02.440 --> 00:29:05.750
And basically what it does
is it keeps a local copy

00:29:05.750 --> 00:29:07.870
of a Firebase database.

00:29:07.870 --> 00:29:08.594
MARK MANDEL: Wow.

00:29:08.594 --> 00:29:10.010
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
And it allows you

00:29:10.010 --> 00:29:12.430
to basically do all
the modifications,

00:29:12.430 --> 00:29:14.640
reads, and writes on it.

00:29:14.640 --> 00:29:17.200
And then once you
get connected again,

00:29:17.200 --> 00:29:19.495
it syncs to the remote database.

00:29:19.495 --> 00:29:21.120
MARK MANDEL: So you
don't have to worry

00:29:21.120 --> 00:29:23.410
about any of that complicated
stuff of syncing data

00:29:23.410 --> 00:29:25.202
back again and managing
conflicts and doing

00:29:25.202 --> 00:29:25.993
that sort of stuff.

00:29:25.993 --> 00:29:27.080
It manages that for you.

00:29:27.080 --> 00:29:28.788
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
and not only that,

00:29:28.788 --> 00:29:32.570
but it also helps you
manage things like presence.

00:29:32.570 --> 00:29:35.087
So if you imagine
that you have a chat,

00:29:35.087 --> 00:29:36.670
you could have
something where you can

00:29:36.670 --> 00:29:39.040
see who is online, who is not.

00:29:39.040 --> 00:29:41.090
You could see if
you online or not.

00:29:41.090 --> 00:29:43.740
And even if you're not, you
can still send messages,

00:29:43.740 --> 00:29:46.270
and those messages will
actually be sent to the chat

00:29:46.270 --> 00:29:49.020
once you get online.

00:29:49.020 --> 00:29:53.820
Even more crazy than that,
you can even do transactions.

00:29:53.820 --> 00:29:55.330
MARK MANDEL: Wow.

00:29:55.330 --> 00:29:56.085
That's crazy.

00:29:56.085 --> 00:29:57.876
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
that's pretty crazy.

00:29:57.876 --> 00:30:00.530
So you can do transactions
on the database,

00:30:00.530 --> 00:30:04.060
and the transactions will be
tried once you get online.

00:30:04.060 --> 00:30:07.470
And they could fail, of course,
but if you have something

00:30:07.470 --> 00:30:13.050
like I just need to
increase this I counter,

00:30:13.050 --> 00:30:16.560
probably if that counter
changed since you were offline,

00:30:16.560 --> 00:30:19.110
it will fail, and you will get
a notification saying, hey,

00:30:19.110 --> 00:30:21.300
that actually failed,
you have to retry.

00:30:21.300 --> 00:30:23.050
But if you have something
different, which

00:30:23.050 --> 00:30:26.730
is like maybe the user decided
to change their username,

00:30:26.730 --> 00:30:28.420
or something of that,
that's something

00:30:28.420 --> 00:30:31.650
that only them can do, so that
was something that probably

00:30:31.650 --> 00:30:34.330
will succeed
whenever you actually

00:30:34.330 --> 00:30:35.710
are connected to the network.

00:30:35.710 --> 00:30:37.418
So that's actually
something really cool.

00:30:37.418 --> 00:30:39.290
It really makes me
think about what

00:30:39.290 --> 00:30:40.710
